---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/expert/causal-inference">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every science wants the same thing: not "these two things move together" but "this one
    moves <em>because of</em> that one". This chapter climbs that ladder in six steps. It
    begins where the bivariate-distributions unit begins — two variables, one scatter
    diagram, one fitted line — and ends where causal inference proper takes over: the
    machinery (potential outcomes, causal diagrams, natural experiments) that lets a
    researcher defend a causal claim from messy observational data. The unit's tools are
    not warm-ups for that machinery; they are its surface. The regression line is the most
    aggressively over-interpreted object in applied science, and the discipline's modern
    history — from statistics' long refusal to say "cause" at all, to Rubin's potential
    outcomes, Pearl's do-calculus and the 2021 Nobel prize — is the story of learning
    exactly which claims a fitted line can carry, and which it never can.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four ideas that turn association into (defensible) causation" widget="equationStep" config={causalCfg} />

  <h2>Independent and dependent variables: naming the direction</h2>
  <p>
    A bivariate analysis begins with an act of theory disguised as bookkeeping: choosing
    which variable is <strong>independent</strong> (the suspected driver, plotted on the
    horizontal axis) and which is <strong>dependent</strong> (the response, plotted on the
    vertical axis). Nothing in the data forces the choice. The same 200 points plotted
    either way describe the observations equally well, but they assert opposite stories —
    "practice raises scores" versus "high scores cause practice". The asymmetry is real even
    in pure arithmetic: the least-squares line of y on x is not the algebraic inverse of the
    line of x on y, because each answers a different conditional question. Regressing
    revision hours (x) on exam score (y) answers "given five hours of practice, what score
    should we expect?"; the reverse regression answers "given a score of 70, how much
    practice is typical?" — a genuinely different quantity, not a rounding of the first.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the direction of causation can be read off the scatter
    if the relationship is strong." It cannot. Time ordering, experiment design or a
    defensible causal argument supplies the direction; the diagram only records the
    association. Correlation of 0.9 in either direction is consistent with x causing y, y
    causing x, or a third variable causing both.
  </p>

  <h2>Direction and strength: what the correlation coefficient measures</h2>
  <p>
    Pearson's correlation coefficient r condenses a scatter to a single number between
    −1 and +1: its sign gives the direction of the <em>linear</em> association, its
    magnitude the tightness of the cloud around a straight line. Values near ±1 mean the
    points hug a line; values near 0 mean no straight-line pattern. Galton's 1886 height
    data — the study that gave "regression" its name — put parent and child height at
    roughly r = 0.5: real, but loose enough that tall parents' children average shorter
    than the parents, and short parents' children taller. That is the deeper content of r:
    in standardised units the slope of the best-fit line <em>is</em> r, so r = 0.5 already
    means the line predicts only half the deviation to be carried through. And r = 0 does
    not certify independence — it certifies only the absence of a <em>linear</em> pattern.
    Data on y = x² collected symmetrically around zero give r essentially 0 while the
    relationship is perfectly deterministic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a correlation near ±1 is nearly a causal proof." Size
    of r measures association, not mechanism. Ice-cream sales and drowning deaths
    correlate strongly across months because summer drives both; the coefficient is
    indifferent to whether the connection is causal, confounded or coincidental.
  </p>

  <h2>Scatter diagrams: the first causal instrument</h2>
  <p>
    Before any coefficient, plot the data — and treat the plot as an instrument, not a
    formality. Curvature, clusters, gaps and outliers are all visible in a scatter and all
    invisible to a single number. The canonical demonstration is Anscombe's quartet (1973):
    four small data sets constructed so that every summary agrees — each has 11 points,
    mean of x = 9.0, mean of y = 7.5, r = 0.816, and the identical fitted line
    y = 3.00 + 0.50x. Plotted, they are four different worlds: one a clean linear cloud,
    one a perfect curve flattened into a line, one with a single outlier dragging the slope,
    one with one leverage point pinning the whole fit. Any of the four yields the same
    correlation, the same line and the same R²; only the scatter diagram tells you which
    story you are in. This is why serious applied work plots first, computes second — and
    why "the plot looks roughly linear" is a checkable assumption, not a decoration.
  </p>

  <h2>Fitting a line by eye and by semi-averages</h2>
  <p>
    The unit's first fitting method is honest about what a line is: a summary, chosen by
    rule. The <strong>semi-averages</strong> method: order the points by x, split them into
    a lower half and an upper half, compute the mean of x and the mean of y in each half,
    and draw the unique straight line through those two mean points. Worked example —
    typing practice (hours, x) against speed (words per minute, y) for eight learners:
    (1, 12), (2, 18), (3, 24), (4, 26), (5, 38), (6, 40), (7, 49), (8, 53). Lower half:
    mean x = 2.5, mean y = 20. Upper half: mean x = 6.5, mean y = 45. Slope =
    (45 − 20) ÷ (6.5 − 2.5) = 6.25 wpm per hour; the line is y = 20 + 6.25(x − 2.5), i.e.
    y ≈ 4.4 + 6.25x. It predicts 35.6 wpm at x = 5 against an observed 38 — the line
    averages away individual noise, which is exactly its job. Splitting at the median x
    (rather than by count) is a common variant; either way the method is a one-pass
    prototype of least squares, and robust to a single wild point in a way naive
    eyeballing is not.
  </p>

  <h2>The equation of the best-fit line</h2>
  <p>
    The least-squares line minimises the sum of squared vertical distances from points to
    line, and its coefficients have a compact anatomy: slope b = r × (s<sub>y</sub> ÷
    s<sub>x</sub>), intercept a = mean of y − b × mean of x. Worked example: revision hours
    against test scores with r = 0.80, s<sub>x</sub> = 5 hours, s<sub>y</sub> = 10 points,
    mean of x = 12, mean of y = 70. Then b = 0.80 × (10 ÷ 5) = 1.6 points per hour, and
    a = 70 − 1.6 × 12 = 50.8: the predicted score with zero revision. Predicted score at
    15 hours: 50.8 + 1.6 × 15 = 74.8. Two disciplines follow. First, the slope is a claim
    about <em>average</em> effect within the observed range — 1.6 points per hour, not 1.6
    for this student. Second, the line explains variation, not persons: an R² of 0.64 (r²
    for our data) says hours account for 64% of the variance in scores, leaving 36% for
    everything else — ability, sleep, luck — which is precisely where confounding enters.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the intercept always means something." It is the
    prediction at x = 0, which may be outside the data (nobody revised for 0 hours here
    and sat the exam) or meaningless (zero height). Report it, but interpret it only where
    the data actually lives.
  </p>

  <h2>Assumptions and the limits of extrapolation</h2>
  <p>
    Every fitted line silently asserts: the relationship is <em>linear</em> over the
    observed range; the deviations around the line behave roughly like independent random
    noise (no time-bunching, no funnel-shaped spread); and the points sampled are
    exchangeable with the ones you will predict for. Extrapolation beyond the observed
    range breaks all three at once, and the failure is not gradual — it is categorical.
    Shoe size against age fits beautifully from ages 3 to 10; extended to age 25 it
    predicts size 17 shoes, because the true relationship levels off where the data ends.
    Real cases follow the same shape: linear fits to a growing population project absurd
    totals within decades; a line fitted to a firm's early growth says nothing about its
    mature size. The rule of thumb is proportionate: an extrapolation of 10% past the
    range is an estimate; 50% past it is a hope; past a known turning point it is wrong by
    construction. The confidence a plot earned inside its range is exactly the confidence
    it cannot export beyond it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a strong correlation makes extrapolation safer." The
    strength of r describes the scatter <em>inside</em> the observed range and says nothing
    whatsoever about the functional form outside it. Some of history's most confident
    failed forecasts — population bombs, resource exhaustion curves — were high-R² linear
    extrapolations of locally curved processes.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Potential outcomes.</strong> Rubin's framework makes the causal claim
    embarrassingly concrete: each unit has two outcomes, the one that occurs if treated
    and the one that occurs if not, and the causal effect is their difference. We observe
    exactly one — the other is the <em>counterfactual</em>. All of applied causal
    inference is estimation of an unobservable, which is why randomisation is gold: in a
    randomised trial, chance assigns treatment, the two groups are exchangeable in
    expectation, and the mean difference estimates the mean causal effect with no model
    for confounders at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Drawing the arrows.</strong> Pearl's directed acyclic graphs encode assumed
    causal structure and compute, mechanically, which variables to condition on. The
    rules are unforgiving: control for a <em>confounder</em> (a common cause), do not
    control for a <em>mediator</em> (part of the effect you would erase — the
    smoking-versus-birth-weight style error), and never control for a
    <em>collider</em> (a variable caused by two others), which manufactures association
    from nothing — Pearl's own example: among movie stars, beauty and talent appear
    negatively correlated, because stardom requires at least one of the two. "Adjust for
    everything in the database" is therefore not caution; it is a way to bias a result.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Design over statistics.</strong> When experiments are impossible, the
    credibility revolution seeks situations where the world randomised for us. Card and
    Krueger's 1994 comparison of fast-food employment across the New Jersey–Pennsylvania
    border around New Jersey's 1992 minimum-wage rise, and Angrist's use of the Vietnam
    draft lottery as an instrument for military service (white veterans lost roughly 15%
    of earnings per year of service, an effect invisible in raw comparisons), both
    exported the logic of the experiment to observational data. Each design carries a
    scope, too: Card and Krueger identify the short-run effect on fast-food employment
    near one border in 1992, not the effect of every minimum-wage rise everywhere — a
    humility the popular summaries usually lose. The 2021 Nobel to Angrist
    and Imbens formalised what such designs recover — and their limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open frontiers.</strong> The two formal cultures — Pearl's graphs and Rubin's
    potential outcomes — still argue about vocabulary while converging in practice.
    Sensitivity analysis (how strong would an unmeasured confounder have to be to erase
    the finding?) is moving from optional to expected. And machine learning is being
    rebuilt around causal targets: methods that estimate heterogeneous effects rather
    than one average slope, treating the fitted line of this unit as the crudest member
    of a growing family.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Pearl, Causality (2009) and The Book of Why (2018); Imbens & Rubin, Causal Inference (2015); Nobel Prize 2021 citations.</p>
</TopicPage>

